p_10", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_13", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_15", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "Node 1006 is almost inert in the window: only 2 events, a single outgoing edge (1006->1532) and one incoming (1075->1006), and those two events fall in widely separated sub-windows (~35.4k-37.2k and ~40.8k-42.6k), so 1006 emits at most one potential seed event. Node 1860 is reached only via in-edges from 153, 743 and 1390. The 2-hop neighbourhood of 1006 (1075, 1028, 1532, 1036, 1643) contains none of 1860's in-neighbours, so no short route exists. Following the directed out-frontier hop by hop, the only reachable chain to 1860 is 1006->1532->1643->358->743->1860: a 5-hop path, all of whose intermediate nodes are extremely low-degree (1532: 3 events, 1643 and 358 each a single out-edge). Requiring four successive hops each within 600 time units, in a window of 14,400 units with only 3,170 events (density ~0.22 events/unit, repeat ratio 0.007), is implausible; the alternative high-traffic hubs (153, 274, 1028) that could shorten the route are not reachable from 1006's out-frontier, ruling out the 'popular-hub' explanation. I therefore find no time-respecting 600-unit cascade from 1006 to 1860; the connection exists only when time is ignored."}, "raw_final_response": "```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_806cc48b\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [1006, 1860, 1532, 1643, 358, 743],\n  \"window\": [30031, 44431],\n  \"confidence\": 0.62,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_16\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"Node 1006 is almost inert in the window: only 2 events, a single outgoing edge (1006->1532) and one incoming (1075->1006), and those two events fall in widely separated sub-windows (~35.4k-37.2k and ~40.8k-42.6k), so 1006 emits at most one potential seed event. Node 1860 is reached only via in-edges from 153, 743 and 1390. The 2-hop neighbourhood of 1006 (1075, 1028, 1532, 1036, 1643) contains none of 1860's in-neighbours, so no short route exists. Following the directed out-frontier hop by hop, the only reachable chain to 1860 is 1006->1532->1643->358->743->1860: a 5-hop path, all of whose intermediate nodes are extremely low-degree (1532: 3 events, 1643 and 358 each a single out-edge). Requiring four successive hops each within 600 time units, in a window of 14,400 units with only 3,170 events (density ~0.22 events/unit, repeat ratio 0.007), is implausible; the alternative high-traffic hubs (153, 274, 1028) that could shorten the route are not reachable from 1006's out-frontier, ruling out the 'popular-hub' explanation. I therefore find no time-respecting 600-unit cascade from 1006 to 1860; the connection exists only when time is ignored.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 39700, "total_output_tokens": 5793, "total_tokens": 45493, "total_latency_ms": 78417, "llm_calls": 7, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}